Minimum Number of Swaps to Make the String Balanced
🔹 Problem Statement

You are given a string s consisting only of '[' and ']'.

A string is balanced if:

Every opening bracket [ has a corresponding closing bracket ]

At no point should closing brackets exceed opening brackets when scanning from left to right

Return the minimum number of swaps required to make the string balanced.

You can swap any two characters.

🔹 Example 1
Input: s = "]]][[["
Output: 2
Explanation:

After 2 swaps, we can make:

"[ ][ ][ ]"

Balanced string.

🔹 Example 2
Input: s = "[]"
Output: 0

Already balanced.

🔎 Intuition

Instead of actually swapping, we observe:

Count imbalance

Every time closing bracket exceeds open bracket → imbalance increases

Every swap fixes 2 imbalance units

👉 So final formula becomes:

(swaps = (imbalance + 1) / 2)

Where:

imbalance = number of unmatched closing brackets
⚙️ Algorithm

Initialize:

open = 0
imbalance = 0

Traverse string:

If [, increment open

If ]:

If open > 0 → match it

Else → imbalance++

Minimum swaps required:

(imbalance + 1) / 2
💻 Java Code
class Solution {
    public int minSwaps(String s) {
        int open = 0;
        int imbalance = 0;
        
        for (char c : s.toCharArray()) {
            
            if (c == '[') {
                open++;
            } else { // ']'
                if (open > 0) {
                    open--;
                } else {
                    imbalance++;
                }
            }
        }
        
        return (imbalance + 1) / 2;
    }
}
🧠 Code Explanation (Step by Step)
Example:
s = "]]][[["
Step-by-step:
Character	open	imbalance
]	0	1
]	0	2
]	0	3
[	1	3
[	2	3
[	3	3

Final:

imbalance = 3
swaps = (3 + 1) / 2 = 2
💡 Why Formula Works?

Each swap fixes two incorrect positions.

Example:

]][[

Swap first ] with last [
→ []][

One swap removes two imbalance issues.

Thus:

needed swaps = ceil(imbalance / 2)
